class.description:
A [FlowGraph] is a resource that describes a flow: nodes ([FlowNode]) with named input and output ports ([FlowPort]), and connections between them. [b]Flow[/b] connections carry the execution from node to node, [b]data[/b] connections carry values. Typical uses are dialogs, quests, tutorials and scripted sequences. The graph is plain data: it needs no scene tree and no threads, and works on every platform including Web. The built-in node types and their ports are listed at [FlowNode]; running a graph is the job of [FlowGraphPlayback] (or [FlowGraphPlayer] in the scene tree). Validation runs at the first [method FlowGraphPlayback.start] of a graph and its issues are printed as warnings.
[b]Node ids.[/b] The graph owns the node ids. [method add_node] returns the id of the new node, and ids are never reused, not even after [method remove_node]; saved ids stay valid for the lifetime of the graph. A node belongs to one graph at a time. The first [FlowStartNode] that is added becomes the start of the graph ([member start_node_id]).
[b]Connections.[/b] Create them with [method connect_nodes], which checks the nodes, the ports, the kinds and the types (see there). A flow output has one connection at most; a data input has one connection at most. A data output can feed many inputs, and a flow input can have many incoming connections. [method validate] checks a whole graph again, which also catches hand-edited files.
[b]Storage.[/b] The properties [member node_ids], [member nodes], [member connections], [member frames] and [member next_node_id] are the saved form of the graph. They are hidden in the inspector and are not meant to be set directly; edit the graph with the methods of this class. Only [member variables] and [member start_node_id] are shown in the inspector.
[b]Saving and copying.[/b] A graph is saved like any resource, for example as [code].tres[/code] or [code].res[/code], and also as JSON with the resource text serializer. When a graph is loaded or duplicated, it only claims the nodes that do not belong to a graph yet. A shallow [code]duplicate()[/code] therefore shares the node objects with the original and leaves them in the original. To copy a graph, use [code]duplicate(true)[/code], which duplicates the nodes too.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var graph := FlowGraph.new()
var start := graph.add_node(FlowStartNode.new(), Vector2(0, 0))
var wait := FlowWaitNode.new()
wait.seconds = 2.0
var wait_id := graph.add_node(wait, Vector2(200, 0))
var end := graph.add_node(FlowEndNode.new(), Vector2(400, 0))
graph.connect_nodes(start, "out", wait_id, "in")
graph.connect_nodes(wait_id, "out", end, "in")
print(graph.validate().is_valid()) # true
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var graph = new FlowGraph();
int start = graph.AddNode(new FlowStartNode(), new Vector2(0, 0));
var wait = new FlowWaitNode { Seconds = 2.0 };
int waitId = graph.AddNode(wait, new Vector2(200, 0));
int end = graph.AddNode(new FlowEndNode(), new Vector2(400, 0));
graph.ConnectNodes(start, "out", waitId, "in");
graph.ConnectNodes(waitId, "out", end, "in");
GD.Print(graph.Validate().IsValid()); // True
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var copy: FlowGraph = graph.duplicate(true)
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var copy = (FlowGraph)graph.Duplicate(true);
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
ResourceSaver.save(graph, "res://graphs/intro.tres")
var loaded := load("res://graphs/intro.tres") as FlowGraph
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
ResourceSaver.Save(graph, "res://graphs/intro.tres");
var loaded = GD.Load<FlowGraph>("res://graphs/intro.tres");
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
